[Music]  the 1990s were a golden age of  television animation  but cartoons are a business and even the  most beloved shows are sometimes  cancelled  to make room for more profitable  programming here's the real reason these  90s cartoons were canceled  in the 1990s fox became the only place  on broadcast tv where primetime  animation could find an audience  and flourish creatively following the  success of the simpsons  the network debuted another very  different show about a cartoon family in  early 1997  king of the hill created by beavis and  butthead mastermind mike judge  the low-key but hilarious series  centered on hank hill  an extremely uptight family man from the  town of arlen texas  who loved beer football and selling  propane he often struggled to relate to  his weird tween son bobby  teaching my boy the facts of life is my  job  right king of the hill earned all kinds  of emmy attention  and was a consistent hit with viewers it  remained on the fox lineup for well over  a decade  but after 259 episodes king of the hill  was out of gas  its final episode to sirloin with love  aired just before the start of the  2009-2010 tv season king of the hill  helped further popularize network  cartoons but ironically  the show was forced to step aside for a  new animated series  shortly after fox announced its final  order of king of the hill episodes  it gave a full season pick up to the  cleveland show  a spin-off of the hit cartoon family guy  before a slew of blockbuster x-men  movies hit the silver screen  kids got to know professor xavier's crew  of super-powered mutants on fox's  saturday morning staple x-men  the animated series based on the marvel  comics that date back to the 60s  the show was mostly serialized with  viewers required to turn in each week to  see how multi-part story lines played  out  x-men the animated series debuted on fox  in 1992  and eventually moved to its weekend  morning time slot where it aired until  1997.  that's a bit longer than the network or  producers had envisioned  the show was only supposed to run 65  episodes  a standard production run for an  animated show in the 90s  fox ultimately wanted a total of 76  episodes  and once those 76 episodes were finished  so was x-men  the animated series i miss you  i never knew how much i would we all  miss  scott the question is how will we move  on without her  doug premiered in 1991 and was one of  the sweetest  gentlest cartoons of its era it was told  from the point of view of awkward tween  doug funny  who makes up for his painful shyness  with a wildly active fantasy life  this wasn't so hard maybe if i kept at  it  nickelodeon ended production on doug in  1994.  shortly after that disney purchased abc  and launched the one saturday morning  programming block  according to doug creator jim jenkins  disney aggressively pursued his  animation studio jumbo pictures  because it wanted doug in slimed and  oral history of nickelodeon's golden age  jenkins asked  so should i stay with nickelodeon who is  through with me  or get bought by disney where we get to  create 65 new half hours of dug  a feature-length movie dug live for  their theme park  toys books additional funding for  development and production on many new  series  in 1996 brand spanking new doug hit abc  more focused on the business side of  doug jenkins wasn't around to maintain  quality and the show arguably went  downhill when others stepped in  casting director wendy litwack said doug  suffered when it changed to disney  because of network intervention  the new doug came to a conclusion at the  end of its third season  followed by a big screen movie  hey arnold joined the nicktoons lineup  in 1996  and quickly became one of nickelodeon's  most popular and enduring  shows for five seasons fans eagerly  tuned in  to watch the many adventures of this  group of highly independent city kids  steel mill weird the steel mill sure  why'd you think it worked this train  isn't haunted  in 1998 nickelodeon renewed hey arnold  and ordered a feature film  which creator greg bartlett began  working on after the fifth season  it was a straight to video project  called arnold saves the neighborhood  but it was also released to theaters as  hey arnold the movie  the movie flopped in 2002 earning only  13 million dollars at the american box  office that marked the end of hey arnold  in any form  until bartlett returned to nickelodeon  to produce hey arnold the jungle movie  in 2016.  roseanne barr ruled abc in the late 80s  and early 90s  roseanne the sitcom based on her  stand-up comedy act  finished the 1989-90 season as the  number one show on network tv  in the fall of 1990 the network tried to  capitalize on her popularity with a  roseanne barr show just for kids  a saturday morning cartoon called little  rosie  little rosie revolved around an  eight-year-old version of barr  nobody from the roseanne sitcom voiced  their animated kitty counterparts not  even barr  abc tried to interfere with the  production early on requesting that  rosie's friend group include more boys  it was reportedly a difficult show to  make and ratings weren't so great  no wonder little rosie only lasted one  season mrs canker  i won't get it on sorry i kicked the  ball into your yard  we don't do it on purpose and we don't  like to bother  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies and tv shows are  coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

